## Git stash policy (worktrees)

This repo is used across multiple `git worktree` checkouts in parallel.
`refs/stash` is SHARED across all worktrees of the same repo — a bare
`git stash pop` or `git stash apply` run in one worktree can pull in another
worktree's stashed changes. This is a real footgun for parallel agent work.

**Rules:**

1. NEVER run `git stash pop` or `git stash apply` without an explicit stash
   reference (e.g. `stash@{2}`). Bare pop/apply is forbidden.
2. Before any stash operation, run `git stash list` and confirm which entry
   actually belongs to the current worktree (check the message/branch it was
   made on) before touching it.
3. Prefer the worktree-scoped helpers below over raw `git stash push/pop`.
   They store the stash under a ref namespaced to the current worktree
   (`refs/wtstash/<worktree-name>`) instead of the shared `refs/stash`, so
   it's structurally impossible to pop the wrong worktree's stash.

   - `gwt-stash-save` — stash current worktree's changes
   - `gwt-stash-pop`  — restore this worktree's stashed changes

   These are fish functions defined in `~/.config/fish/functions/`.

4. If you (the agent) need to stash uncommitted changes as part of a task,
   use `gwt-stash-save` / `gwt-stash-pop`, never raw `git stash`.
5. If these helpers aren't available in the current shell (e.g. a non-fish
   subshell), STOP and ask before running any `git stash pop`/`apply` —
   do not guess which stash index is correct.

## Git worktree isolation for agent work

Do all substantive edit/commit work in a per-task `git worktree`, not in a
repo's live/primary checkout — for every repo, not just the ones where this
has bitten before.

**Why:** the user often does their own git work (branch switches, commits,
`pull --ff-only`, releases) in the same working copy while an agent is
mid-task. A single working directory has one HEAD — concurrent branch
switches or `git reset`/`checkout` collide, and this has repeatedly clobbered
uncommitted agent work or the user's own in-progress changes (files "missing",
edits silently reverted, changes mid-read).

**How to apply:** `git fetch origin -q && git worktree add <path> -b <branch>
origin/main` (or pass `isolation: "worktree"` to the Agent tool), do the work
there, commit on the branch, rebase onto the latest `origin/main` before
pushing, then remove the worktree. If you must work in the shared checkout,
re-verify the current branch (`git branch --show-current`) before every
edit/commit — never assume your branch is still checked out across turns.
Use the `worktree-task` skill for the create/finish/cleanup steps rather than
improvising them by hand.

## Git hygiene

- **Bash's cwd persists across tool calls.** When juggling multiple repos in
  one session, never rely on ambient cwd for a mutating git command — a stray
  earlier `cd` can retarget a commit into the wrong repo. Put the `cd` and the
  git command in the same call (`cd /path/to/repo && git commit ...`) or use
  `git -C /path/to/repo ...`.
- **Run `git status` (or `git diff --cached --stat`) before every `git
  commit`**, even after `git add <one-file>` — `git add` is additive and does
  not un-stage anything staged earlier in the session (possibly unrelated
  work). Prefer explicit file lists over `git add -A`/`-u`/`.`.
- **Never force-push a git tag.** If a release needs a fix after tagging, cut
  a new incremented version tag instead. Force-pushing a tag can trigger
  duplicate/failed release runs and leaves registries/consumers uncertain
  which build they actually got.
- **In repos where a `v*` tag drives CI build/publish, never chain `git commit
  && git tag vX && git push origin vX` in one shot.** A commit-msg hook can
  reject the commit; the shell still proceeds to `git tag`, which then tags
  the OLD HEAD — CI builds/publishes the new version number from stale code.
  Commit first as its own step, verify `git log --oneline -1 HEAD` actually
  advanced, only then tag and push. Verify the published artifact itself
  (image sha / package version), not just "publish: success" — some
  registries (npm) are immutable and a bad publish can't be overwritten,
  only superseded by the next version. Use the `tag-release` skill for this
  end-to-end rather than chaining the steps by hand.
- **Commit-msg hooks in git-templates-managed repos enforce a subject ≤50
  chars** (conventional-commit prefix, e.g. `feat(x): short subject`) —
  write the bare subject with no emoji; a prepare-commit-msg hook
  auto-prepends the gitmoji afterward, so a manually-added emoji prefix
  eats into the 50-char budget and can push you over.
- **Omit the `Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer on commits landing in a repo
  the user doesn't own** (upstream OSS, third-party forks being contributed
  back, external PRs) — maintainers can reject PRs advertising AI
  co-authorship. Keep the trailer on the user's own repos. Check `git remote
  get-url origin` and the PR base to tell which case applies; when unsure,
  ask before adding it (fixing it after the fact needs an amend + force-push).

## Before pushing / declaring CI green

- **Run the project's lint/format/test commands locally before every push** —
  don't rely on CI to catch the first round of failures. Match the CI
  commands exactly (same flags/toolchain).
- **`gh run watch --exit-status` can report exit 0 for a run that actually
  concluded `failure`.** After watching, explicitly verify:
  `gh run view <run-id> --json conclusion --jq .conclusion` and check it's
  `success` before proceeding (e.g. before a downstream manifest/version
  bump).
- **Scripted bulk edits (`str.replace` via a heredoc/script) silently no-op
  when the target text doesn't match** (e.g. after a formatter reformatted
  it). Assert each replacement actually landed rather than trusting exit
  code 0.
- **NEVER pipe a mutating command through `tail`/`head`/`grep` to trim its
  output** — `git push … 2>&1 | tail -2`, `git commit … | tail -1`,
  `kubectl apply … | tail -5`. A pipeline's exit status is the LAST
  command's, so `tail` reports **0** however the real command ended, and the
  trimmed log discards the error message: the failure is silent in both
  channels. Run it bare; if output really must be trimmed, `set -o pipefail`
  first or write to a file and tail the FILE. Then verify the EFFECT, not
  the exit code — `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/<branch>` after a push,
  `git log --oneline -1` after a commit. (Cost 2026-08-20: several pushes
  reported "exited with code 0" and had never left the machine; a long
  pre-push test gate had been killed mid-run under parallel-agent load, and
  `| tail -2` masked it completely.)
- **Before declaring any web-deploy/UI task done, load the live/rendered
  result in a real browser** (Chrome MCP, or headless Playwright as
  fallback) and check it's actually correct — don't stop at CI green, a
  passing unit test, or a bundle grep. A workflow's `conclusion: success`
  only means it didn't throw; the artifact it produced can still ship
  broken, or a slow reconcile/rollout means the fix isn't live yet.
- **When the user reports a visual bug, reproduce their exact view first**
  (same route/mode/zoom/state) before writing a fix, and again after
  deploying. Their words describe what they see, not which code path
  renders it — two branches can look identical in a screenshot, so
  inferring the code path from the description alone risks fixing the
  wrong branch.

## Repo ownership / PR workflow

Work lands via **pull request**, on the user's own repos (`fredericrous/*`)
as much as anywhere else. A `pre-push-branch-protect` git hook rejects a push
to `main` outright ("Pushing to branch main is forbidden. Open a Pull
Request."), so branch + PR is the only route — don't reach for
`git push --no-verify` to get around it.

The standing rule of confirming before destructive/hard-to-reverse operations
(force-push, amending shared history, dropping branches) applies everywhere,
including the user's own repos.

**Never use `gh pr merge --auto` on the user's repos.** None of them have
branch protection requiring status checks before merge, so `--auto` merges
the PR immediately — before CI, including tests, has even started — instead
of waiting for checks to go green like it does on a protected repo. To
"merge when green": open the PR, poll `gh run view <run-id> --json
status,conclusion` (or `gh pr checks <n>`) until `status=completed` and
`conclusion=success`, THEN merge as a separate command. Never chain the poll
and the merge in one compound command — read the poll result first. Use the
`merge-when-green` skill for this instead of improvising the poll loop.

## Forgejo API ({{ .forge.host }})

The self-hosted forge is fully usable from this machine — opening/merging PRs,
reading CI, repo admin — with **two** credentials, and BOTH are required:

- the **mTLS client cert** at `~/.config/mtls/{{ .forge.slug }}-client.{crt,key}`
  (the gateway enforces `optional: false`; without it curl dies at TLS with
  `LibreSSL SSL_read ... error:1404C45C` and `%{http_code}` **000** — that is
  a missing cert, NOT an auth rejection);
- the **repo-scoped token in `~/.netrc`** (`machine {{ .forge.host }} login
  daddy password <token>`), read with
  `awk '/machine {{ .forge.host }}/{print $6}' ~/.netrc`.

```sh
TOK=$(awk '/machine {{ .forge.host }}/{print $6}' ~/.netrc)
curl -sS --cert ~/.config/mtls/{{ .forge.slug }}-client.crt \
        --key  ~/.config/mtls/{{ .forge.slug }}-client.key \
        -H "Authorization: token $TOK" \
        https://{{ .forge.host }}/api/v1/repos/fredericrous/<repo>/pulls
```

**The trap that cost an hour: do NOT probe `/api/v1/user` to test the token.**
That endpoint needs `read:user`, a scope this token does not carry, so it 403s
with `token does not have at least one of required scope(s): [read:user]` —
which reads like a dead token and says nothing about repository access. Probe
the endpoint you actually need (`/repos/{owner}/{repo}`) instead.

Never echo the token into output, a commit, or a PR body — pass it through the
`$TOK` variable only (see the URL-secret rule under Security).

Details that don't belong here — the CI-log recipe (Forgejo has **no** log
API), run-duration reading, AGit caveats — are in the `reference-forgejo-*`
memories.

## Shell environment

The Bash tool runs commands through **zsh 5.9** (`/bin/zsh`), not bash and
not fish. Fish is only the login shell shown in the environment header; the
tool never uses it (verified 2026-09-09: `ps -p $$` → `/bin/zsh`, and 32,555
transcript calls carry zsh's `(eval):N:` diagnostics and zero fish ones).
Write bash-compatible syntax. Never write fish syntax (`set x (cmd)`,
`for i in (seq 3); …; end`) — under zsh `(cmd)` is a glob and fails with
`no matches found`.

The zsh traps that actually bite, each verified live:

- **`==` as a bare word aborts the whole script.** zsh's `EQUALS` option
  turns a word starting with `=` into a command lookup: `echo === sep ===`
  → `== not found`, and `[ "$a" == "b" ]` or `test x == y` →
  `= not found`, and every clause after it is skipped. This is the
  wait-loop killer: `until [ "$st" == "completed" ]; do …` dies on its
  first iteration. Use `=` inside `[ ]`/`test`, or `[[ $a == b ]]` (fine
  inside `[[ ]]`), and `---`/`###` for separators.
- **A zero-match glob aborts the clause** (`NOMATCH`, on by default):
  `no matches found: --include=*.yaml`. It fires INSIDE flag values too,
  and the abort is per clause — in a `;` chain the next clause still runs
  and the tool reports success, so the grep you wanted silently never ran
  (measured: 86–95% of these came back green). Quote globs in flag values
  (`--include='*.yaml'`), and prefer `find`/`git ls-files` over relying on
  shell glob output.
- **`status` is read-only** (`status=$(cmd)` → `read-only variable:
  status`). `argv`, `version`, `PWD`, `pipestatus` are writable in zsh —
  the longer read-only list was fish's. Prefer `ci_state`/`rc` anyway.

- **`grep` is not grep, `find` is not find.** Claude Code's shell snapshot
  runs `grep` as ugrep with `--ignore-files`: a recursive grep SILENTLY
  skips every .gitignored path (dist/, node_modules/, build output) — exit
  0, no message, even with no `.git` dir. Name the directory explicitly
  (`grep -rn X node_modules/pkg` does search it), pass `--no-ignore-files`,
  or use `command grep`. A missing path is a `ugrep: warning:` mid-stream,
  not a fatal error, so a search that read nothing looks like one that found
  nothing. `find` is bfs, a GNU superset — `-printf`, `-newermt` all work.
- **Tool flavors on PATH:** GNU sed and full GNU coreutils come first
  (`stat -c`, `date -d`, `readlink -f`, `sha256sum` all GNU); awk, xargs,
  tar, column and diff are BSD. `sed -i ''` and `stat -f '%…'` are the two
  BSD spellings that fail here — the latter succeeds into a wrong answer.

`&&`/`||` chaining, heredocs, `$(cmd)`, `[[ ]]`, `for …; do … done`,
`while`/`until`, `$((…))`, brace expansion, `local`/`export`, `2>&1 |`
all work as in bash — none of them produced a single diagnostic in six
weeks of transcripts.

## Reading files and searching (overrides the bypass-mode default)

Measured 2026-09-09 over 41,700 tool calls: files opened through Bash
(`cat`, `sed -n`, `head`) were 31% of all tool-result bytes, re-reads of a
file already read in the same session another 15%, and the Grep/Glob tools
were used zero times. The harness's bypass-permissions prompt says to prefer
Bash over the dedicated tools; this section overrides it for reading.

- **Open files with the Read tool, not `cat`/`sed -n`/`head`.** Read has
  line numbers, a size cap and `offset`/`limit`; a `cat` dumps the whole file
  into context with none of that.
- **Read with `offset`/`limit` on anything over ~300 lines.** Grep for the
  anchor first (Grep tool, or `grep -n … | head`), then read a window around
  it. The four largest results in the window were unbounded Reads of 67–86 KB.
- **Search with the Grep and Glob tools** when the answer is a location; use
  Bash `grep`/`rg` only for pipelines (`| wc -l`, `| sort | uniq -c`).
- **Do not re-read a file you already have in context** unless it was edited
  since. Edit/Write results confirm the change; re-reading to verify is the
  15% bucket.
- **When a tool result was persisted to a file** ("Output too large … saved
  to …"), `grep`/`head -c` the saved file for the part you need. Reading it
  back whole pays for the output twice (31 of 83 cases did).
- Bash is still right for anything that runs a program, and `cat` is fine
  inside a pipe or a heredoc.

## Claude Code agent tooling

When the session model is a Fable/Mythos `[1m]` (1M-context) variant,
background `Agent` spawns that inherit it can fail at launch ("model may not
exist or you may not have access") — sometimes only after the agent has
already done real work, so the work is lost, not just the spawn. For any
non-trivial background agent, pass an explicit `model` override (e.g.
`opus`) instead of inheriting. If a spawn fails mid-flight, check the
worktree/branch it was using before assuming the work is gone — a stalled
agent often leaves a clean, compiling partial result worth resuming from
rather than restarting.

## Security

When a secret rides in a URL (webhook URL, presigned S3 URL, any `?token=`
query param), guarding your own log calls is not enough — silence the HTTP
client library's own request logging too, since libraries like `httpx`,
`urllib3`, and `boto3`/`botocore` log the full request URL at INFO by
default. Clamp those loggers to WARNING at the entry point
(`logging.getLogger("httpx").setLevel(logging.WARNING)`) so transport
failures stay visible but the URL-embedded credential doesn't. The same
applies anywhere a dependency echoes something you passed it back out
(exception reprs, tracebacks, OTEL span attributes) — not just logging.

## Communication style

Surface an open action item (e.g. "you still need to rotate credential X") at
most once, or again only when its context materially changes. Repeating a
known open item in every summary reads as nagging. Track it in memory if it
matters; don't re-mention it in every reply.
